  "slug": "22q11-deletion-syndrome-c722",
  "summary": "iPSC and mouse models now link mitophagy, oligodendrocyte and blood-brain barrier changes to variable penetrance in 22q11.2 deletion.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is cellular mechanisms associated with variable schizophrenia penetrance in 22q11.2 deletion syndrome. In a deletion mouse model, prefrontal cortex at P28 shows increased immature and mature oligodendrocytes with reduced myelination. In iPSC-derived neurons from deletion carriers, schizophrenia is associated with impaired mitophagy and lysosomal acidity, a defect described as reversible. iPSC-derived blood-brain barrier cells from carriers show structural and metabolic insufficiency associated with penetrance. What is new is the linkage of penetrance-associated differences to specific cellular states across neuronal, glial and barrier compartments. A cntnap2-/- autism model reporting oligodendroglial dysfunction and PV interneuron hypomyelination is a separate context and is not attributed to the deletion.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-22"
}
